{{also|punchout|punch-out}}
==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To repeatedly [[hit]] a person.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To hit a person so that they become [[unconscious]] ([[knocked out]]).
#: {{ux|en|I'm going to '''punch out''' that [[SOB]] and leave him lying on the ground bleeding.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To use a [[punch]] to remove a piece of material or to remove a piece already [[scored]].
#: {{ux|en|I spent all day '''punching out''' the die cut decals.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|US}} To leave a workplace by punching a [[timecard]].
#: {{ant|en|clock in#Verb|clock on#Verb|punch in#Verb}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm going to '''punch out''' a bit early today.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To leave a workplace.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|aviation}} To [[eject]] from an airplane.
#: {{ux|en|With the engine on fire he had to '''punch out''' and hit the silk.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|computing}} To extract {{l|en|data}} from a computer by the use of a {{l|en|keyboard}}.
# {{lb|en|transitive|baseball}} To rule (by an [[umpire]]) that a pitch is a called third strike, often done emphatically.
#: {{ux|en|The ump '''punched out''' the batter with relish to close out the game.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|baseball|of a [[pitcher]]}} To throw a called third [[strike]]; to [[strike out#English:_baseball|strike (someone) out]].
#: {{ux|en|Jones '''punched out''' the batter to start in the sixth.}}

